<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What Android's Game Mode is allowed to do to this game.

    Declaring android:appCategory="game" is what makes the system treat
    Pitchpole as a game at all: the Game Dashboard appears, and battery and
    data usage are attributed under Games rather than under apps. This file is
    the second half of that — it says which of the system's interventions are
    welcome.

    Every answer here comes from one fact about this game: it is thirty seconds
    of tight timing windows, and it renders almost nothing to achieve that. A
    level is flat vector shapes on a fixed 560 by 220 viewport, so there is
    very little GPU work to take away and very little battery to win back.

    supportsPerformanceGameMode
        Yes. Smooth frames are the whole feel of a runner, and section 12 of
        the build document budgets real time on exactly that.

    supportsBatteryGameMode
        No, and this is the one worth explaining rather than a default. Battery
        mode saves power by throttling the frame rate, and the simulation is
        safe from that — it runs on a fixed 1/120 second timestep with an
        accumulator, so physics is identical at 30, 60 or 120 frames per second
        and no tuned hop window can drift. What is not safe is the input. Taps
        are queued and drained once per frame, so a throttled frame rate is a
        throttled input sampling rate, and a game whose difficulty is
        deliberately built out of tight windows becomes harder in a way the
        player never agreed to. Offering a battery mode that had to be refused
        this intervention would be offering a mode that does nothing.

    allowGameDownscaling
        No. The game already renders to a fixed virtual resolution and is
        letterboxed to fit, so downscaling buys no meaningful GPU time and
        costs a blurrier picture.

    allowGameFpsOverride
        No, for the input reason above.
-->
<game-mode-config
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:supportsPerformanceGameMode="true"
    android:supportsBatteryGameMode="false"
    android:allowGameDownscaling="false"
    android:allowGameFpsOverride="false" />
